| [ADR-072](../../../record/decisions.d/ADR-072.md) | The status note is its own field | `status: Superseded — by FX-ADR-032` was one scalar carrying two types: a word from a closed vocabulary, and a prose note that was rendered, rebased for links, and split off the word in six places. The note is now its own field, `status_note:`, and a prose key like `summary:` — a code in it is a citation the fixer links. The successor a superseded document names is a reference field, `superseded_by:` (ADR-071); the note is for what the field cannot say. A note still riding in `status:` is a lint finding that `luria index` repairs, the way it fills `created:` from a path ([ADR-031](../../../record/decisions.d/ADR-031.md)). Amends [ADR-003](../../../record/decisions.d/ADR-003.md), which placed the note after an em-dash. Rejected: leaving the scalar and parsing it forever, which keeps the field's type a lie; and scanning `status:` as prose without splitting it. | Active |
| [ADR-073](../../../record/decisions.d/ADR-073.md) | A finding names the key that declared its obligation; the record page lists the contract; no explain verb | A lint finding said "(luria.toml)" and no more, which is one file today and the wrong table the day a second authoring surface exists. Findings now cite the key that declared the obligation, the generated record page lists each scheme's whole contract from the same renderer, and there is no `luria explain` verb: provenance belongs where a surprised author meets it, and [ADR-030](../../../record/decisions.d/ADR-030.md) retired the last standalone report commands nobody ran. Rejected: the verb, a verbose lint mode, and a separate explanation renderer that would drift from the findings. | Active |
